    <string name="nutrition_score_how">How it works</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_title">How the nutrition score works</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_intro">Foods are rated with Nutri-Score, the label used on packaging in France, Germany, Belgium and other EU countries. The app uses its 2023 version, which follows dietary guidelines more closely.</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_against_title">What counts against a food</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_against">Calories, sugar, saturated fat and salt, per 100 g (or 100 ml for drinks).</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_for_title">What counts for it</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_for">Protein, fiber, and the share of fruit, vegetables, legumes and nuts. Protein doesn\'t count for foods already high in calories, sugar, fat or salt, except cheese.</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_groups_title">Food groups</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_groups">Each group has its own scale. Drinks: only plain water gets an A, and milk counts as a drink. Fats, oils, nuts and seeds: judged by how much of the fat is saturated, so olive oil can get a B. Cheese: protein always counts. Red meat: protein counts for less. Alcoholic drinks aren\'t rated.</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_scale_title">From letter to score</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_scale">Lists are sorted by a score from 0 to 100. Each letter has its own range, and the exact points decide the place within it, so two B foods still rank. Equal scores go by protein and fiber per 100 kcal.</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_badges_title">Filled and outlined badges</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_badges">A filled badge is the official grade from Open Food Facts. An outlined badge is calculated by the app from the nutrition values.</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_missing_title">When data is missing</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_missing">If sugar or saturated fat is unknown, the food is rated at both extremes (none of it, or all of its carbs or fat) and placed in between. The food sheet then shows the possible range, such as \"could be B–D\". If more than three letters are possible, no letter is shown.</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_limits_title">Limits</string>
    <string name="nutrition_score_limits">The app can\'t see sweeteners or the exact vegetable share of a ready meal, so a calculated grade can be slightly worse than the official one. A score compares similar foods; it isn\'t a verdict on your whole diet.</string>
    <string name="nutrient_sugar_per">Sugar/%1$s</string>
